WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.512
Applications need secrets at the moment they connect, sign, decrypt, or authenticate.

2
00:00:05.512 --> 00:00:13.036
The delivery pattern should make the secret available to the authorized workload without spreading it into places that

3
00:00:13.036 --> 00:00:14.824
are hard to control.

4
00:00:14.824 --> 00:00:21.454
Common patterns include fetching a secret at startup, retrieving it at runtime through a secret manager,

5
00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:27.190
mounting it into a container through an orchestrator, using a platform-managed identity,

6
00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.691
or receiving a short-lived token from a trusted broker.

7
00:00:30.691 --> 00:00:33.149
Runtime access can reduce hardcoding.

8
00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:36.576
Instead of storing a database password in source code,

9
00:00:36.576 --> 00:00:42.014
the application can read it from an approved secret manager using a workload identity.

10
00:00:42.014 --> 00:00:48.867
Instead of placing a cloud key in a deployment manifest, the workload can use managed identity or federation.

11
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:54.454
These approaches keep static secrets out of code and make access policy easier to manage,

12
00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:59.520
but they still require careful configuration, monitoring, and error handling.

13
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.479
Environment variables are common because they are easy for platforms and pipelines to inject.

14
00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:09.725
They can be acceptable in some environments, but they are not magic.

15
00:01:09.725 --> 00:01:16.504
Depending on the platform, environment values may appear in process inspection, crash dumps, debug pages,

16
00:01:16.504 --> 00:01:20.452
support bundles, logs, metrics, or administrative consoles.

17
00:01:20.452 --> 00:01:22.836
If a team uses environment variables,

18
00:01:22.836 --> 00:01:30.285
it should understand where those values can travel and should avoid dumping the environment during troubleshooting.

19
00:01:30.285 --> 00:01:32.520
CI/CD pipelines need special care.

20
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:39.224
Pipeline variables should be scoped to the minimum project, branch, environment, and job that needs them.

21
00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:45.109
Logs should be masked, but masking is not enough if a tool writes the value to a file or artifact.

22
00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:52.111
Protected branches, approvals, environment gates, and short-lived deployment credentials can reduce risk.

23
00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:57.847
Pull requests from untrusted contexts should not receive production deployment secrets.

24
00:01:57.847 --> 00:02:01.721
Containers and images introduce another delivery boundary.

25
00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:06.414
A secret used during build should not be preserved in the final image layer.

26
00:02:06.414 --> 00:02:12.969
A runtime secret should not be copied into the application directory or included in a diagnostic bundle.

27
00:02:12.969 --> 00:02:16.321
Mounted secrets should have tight file permissions,

28
00:02:16.321 --> 00:02:23.696
and applications should read them intentionally rather than recursively printing configuration for convenience.

29
00:02:23.696 --> 00:02:27.644
Applications themselves must be designed not to leak secrets.

30
00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:33.976
Do not log authorization headers, raw tokens, private keys, passwords, or full connection strings.

31
00:02:33.976 --> 00:02:36.583
Do not return secrets in error responses.

32
00:02:36.583 --> 00:02:39.116
Do not cache secrets longer than needed.

33
00:02:39.116 --> 00:02:43.586
Do not include secrets in telemetry attributes or exception messages.

34
00:02:43.586 --> 00:02:49.992
Good delivery gets the secret to the workload; good application behavior keeps it from leaving again.
